# Git push identity gate (mandatory)

Before **any** `git push` (including `-u`, tags, or force), run these checks in the repo and compare outputs **exactly** to the expected values. Do **not** push if any check fails — stop and report the mismatch to the user.

## Required checks (run in order)

```bash
git config user.name
# expected: leviacicig

git config user.email
# expected: eviacici@126.com

git remote -v
# expected: at least one remote URL contains github.com-leviacicig
#        OR: git config --get core.sshCommand is set and points at this identity's key

ssh -T git@github.com-leviacicig
# expected: successful GitHub auth for leviacicig
#           (e.g. "Hi leviacicig! You've successfully authenticated...")
```

## Pass / fail rules

| Check | Pass when | Fail when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `user.name` | Exact string `leviacicig` | Empty, different, or missing |
| `user.email` | Exact string `eviacici@126.com` | Empty, different, or missing |
| `remote -v` / SSH | URL contains `github.com-leviacicig`, **or** `core.sshCommand` uses this identity's key | Neither condition holds |
| `ssh -T` | Auth succeeds as `leviacicig` | Permission denied, wrong user, host unreachable, or non-zero without success message |

## On failure

1. **Do not** run `git push` (or any equivalent that updates the remote).
2. Report which check failed, show **expected vs actual**, and wait for the user.
3. Do not "fix" identity by rewriting global git config unless the user explicitly asks.

## On success

Only then may you push. Prefer the existing remote/SSH host (`github.com-leviacicig`) already validated above.
